WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.900
Bounds safety means code reads and writes only within the range that belongs to the object or buffer being used.

2
00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:12.280
In C++, the safest designs keep data and size information together.

3
00:00:12.380 --> 00:00:21.660
Standard containers such as std::vector, std::array, and std::string make the valid range available through their interfaces.

4
00:00:21.760 --> 00:00:28.240
Range-based loops and standard algorithms can reduce the amount of manual index management that reviewers must verify.

5
00:00:28.520 --> 00:00:34.760
Views are useful when code needs non-owning access without losing bounds information.

6
00:00:34.860 --> 00:00:41.100
A std::span can represent a contiguous sequence while preserving the pointer and length as a single value.

7
00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:52.080
That is usually easier to reason about than passing a raw pointer in one parameter and a length in another, especially when the values are transformed, forwarded, or validated in different places.

8
00:00:52.180 --> 00:00:56.100
Views still have lifetimes, so they must not outlive the data they describe.

9
00:00:56.380 --> 00:01:05.100
Raw arrays, unchecked indexes, pointer arithmetic, mismatched lengths, and assumptions about null termination all increase risk.

10
00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:11.360
Defensive code validates sizes before copy, parse, transform, or allocation work.

11
00:01:11.460 --> 00:01:15.060
It checks that offsets and counts are consistent with the available data.

12
00:01:15.160 --> 00:01:24.360
It treats external input, file formats, network messages, and legacy APIs as places where length assumptions deserve special attention.

13
00:01:24.460 --> 00:01:27.740
Bounds-aware code is not just about preventing crashes.

14
00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:35.680
It also makes maintenance safer because the relationship between data and its valid range remains visible across ordinary changes.

15
00:01:35.960 --> 00:01:40.360
When reviewing bounds-sensitive code, follow the data from input to use.

16
00:01:40.460 --> 00:01:47.660
Identify where the size is established, where it is checked, where it may change, and where it controls memory access.

17
00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:56.000
Be cautious when signed and unsigned values mix, when multiplication affects allocation size, or when one buffer length is assumed to describe another buffer.

18
00:01:56.100 --> 00:02:02.260
Small validation choices often decide whether a later operation is straightforward or fragile.
